Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["EquidistanceQ", "ObjectName",
 CellID->1224892054,ExpressionUUID->"8393d601-4014-44ba-afc9-60862df940ec"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell["   ", "ModInfo",ExpressionUUID->"797591f7-cd7c-47a5-a2b1-7073355d1ef9"],
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["EquidistanceQ"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/EquidistanceQ", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["g", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["a", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["b", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["c", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["d", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "11503a08-e398-4543-be33-24fe02fe9066"],
 "\[LineSeparator]",
 "tests Tarski's equidistance ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["a", "TI"], 
    StyleBox["b", "TI"], "\[Congruent]", 
    StyleBox["c", "TI"], 
    StyleBox["d", "TI"]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "96493f94-3c17-401d-8ae5-02c7f264d7b0"],
 ": whether ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{
      StyleBox["a", "TI"], ",", 
      StyleBox["b", "TI"]}], ")"}], "\[LongEqual]", 
    StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{
      StyleBox["c", "TI"], ",", 
      StyleBox["d", "TI"]}], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "fd3e2e41-1d40-44ae-95df-cfb333266ceb"],
 " in ",
 StyleBox["g", "TI"],
 ".",
 "\n"
}], "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"a446117f-3697-40eb-9f1a-56fa68212f6d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Equidistance is the second primitive of Tarski's axiomatization, alongside \
betweenness (",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["BetweennessQ"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/BetweennessQ", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "068c1d44-29d9-4256-8cfe-2be7550d54d2"],
 "). It is a ",
 StyleBox["four-place",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " relation on vertices \[LongDash] it compares two segments rather than \
describing one \[LongDash] and it is what does the work of congruence: Tarski \
has no separate congruence primitive, no motions, and no ruler."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"2d6216b5-90ff-4558-b885-c505d6c5be5c"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "On a graph it reduces to equality of graph distances, so it is cheap. What \
is not cheap, and not automatic, is that the axioms governing it hold: ",
 Cell[BoxData["TarskiCongruenceReflexivityQ"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "9eff04cf-aaa1-44fd-b174-ae36b0493749"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData["TarskiCongruenceTransitivityQ"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "be102473-239e-44da-ab4a-afcd0b1052b5"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData["TarskiCongruenceIdentityQ"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "3978384c-cde6-4b40-9bc6-f895186af23d"],
 " test the three that concern equidistance alone, and ",
 Cell[BoxData["TarskiAxiomQ"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "71da29b8-9ee7-441d-ae44-15b63c73f037"],
 " runs the whole dashboard."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"aa47c78c-fa31-404d-96fe-30161d9eefa9"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Because the graph metric takes only integer values, the equidistance \
classes are coarse: every pair at distance ",
 StyleBox["k", "TI"],
 " is congruent to every other. ",
 Cell[BoxData["TarskiEquidistanceClasses"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "464a2e12-bf03-4c1b-a146-0eae7f77107c"],
 " returns that partition. This is the sharpest way in which a graph is not a \
Euclidean plane \[LongDash] there are only ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["diam",
     FontSlant->"Plain"], "(", 
    StyleBox["g", "TI"], ")"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "a6b29ea2-9201-4c7b-be7c-96c90b5ad4ac"],
 " congruence classes of segments, so congruence carries far less information \
than it does classically."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"08c9629a-283b-44bf-a376-db8924a03a03"],

Cell["Corresponding notions in the classical axiom systems:", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"bbee092e-e249-4966-80bc-961303e1c5ce"],

